/*
    UICrosshair.uss
    Centers the crosshair icon in the viewport and declares the shapes it can take.
*/

.crosshair-root {
    flex-grow: 1;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

/*
    8px, not the 32px this was built at. The uGUI crosshair was an 8x8 rect against a
    1680-wide reference; PanelSettings references 1200, so a point here is 1.4x larger on
    screen and 32 rendered roughly four times the original. A crosshair is a sighting mark —
    growing it is a functional change, not a cosmetic one.

    The size here is the DEFAULT, not the value in force: UITKCrosshair writes an inline width
    and height from ClientCrosshairSettings, which starts at this same 8 so an untouched
    install looks identical either way. It stays declared so the element has a size before the
    panel's OnStarting has run, and so the UI validation scenes render something.
*/
.crosshair-icon {
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-to-fit;
}

/*
    One class per shape. The style modifier is what the setting selects; only one is ever
    applied at a time and UITKCrosshair removes the other two.

    Colour is NOT set here for any of them. The crosshair is a themed colour — UITKThemeManager
    writes `color` and `unityBackgroundImageTintColor` onto .crosshair-icon from the player's
    palette — and the two box-drawn shapes take that same colour as an inline background or
    border from C#. Declaring a colour here as well would win over nothing and mislead the next
    reader into thinking this file decides it.
*/

/*
    The shipped sprite. Crosshair.png is a project-owned 8x8 white plus; the theme system tints
    it at runtime via unityBackgroundImageTintColor (UITKThemeManager.SetTint), so it must stay
    white in the source asset.

    This is the class that carries the image, rather than .crosshair-icon itself. The uGUI
    crosshair drew Unity's BUILT-IN UISprite (fileID 10905), which has no asset to reference
    from USS — that reference is what did not survive the UITK port, leaving the element a
    fully transparent box.
*/
.crosshair-icon--cross {
    background-image: url("Crosshair.png");
}

/* A filled circle. The background colour is written inline from the theme. */
.crosshair-icon--dot {
    background-image: none;
    border-radius: 50%;
}

/*
    An unfilled ring, which leaves the target itself unobscured. Border width is written inline
    so it tracks the chosen size — a fixed 1px ring reads as a dot at 4px and as a hairline at
    32.
*/
.crosshair-icon--circle {
    background-image: none;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    border-radius: 50%;
}
